$DIR/main.p:18:2: cannot call f.Drop(): f is a *File, which only points at a File that something else owns
    Drop releases what an owner holds. The File f points to belongs to another
    variable or place, which will drop it when its life ends, so dropping it
    here would release it twice.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    To end an owner's life early, call Drop on the variable that holds it; it
    cannot be used afterwards:
        v.Drop() // v is the File variable itself
    To leave an empty value behind instead:
        old := take(f)
    (see "Drops" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.6)
    (run "pluto explain E3307" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:27:9: cannot copy the elements of bufs in a range loop: Buf is an owner type
    A range loop copies each element into b. Copying an owner would leave two
    values that both release what it owns, and the element in bufs would still
    believe it owns it.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Range over the indexes and use a pointer to each element:
        for i := range bufs {
            b := &bufs[i]
            ...
        }
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3304" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:32:2: cannot copy elements of type Buf: each element is an owner
    copy duplicates elements, and duplicating an owner would leave two values
    that both release what it owns.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Move the elements one at a time:
        for i := range src {
            dst[i] = take(&src[i])
        }
    or copy each one explicitly:
        for i := range src {
            dst[i] = src[i].Clone()
        }
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3306" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:35:7: cannot assert x to Buf: x is an any, which only lends the value it holds
    Converting an owner to an ordinary interface such as any lends it: the
    interface looks at the owner, which still belongs to the variable that was
    converted. Taking a Buf out of x would create a second owner.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Call the value's methods through an interface type instead:
        if s, ok := x.(fmt.Stringer); ok {
            use(s.String())
        }
    or move it into an owning interface in the first place:
        type Text owner interface{ String() string }
    (see "Type assertions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.13)
    (run "pluto explain E3308" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:39:11: cannot read v[0]: the elements of v are owners, and v[0] would be a copy
    v[0] on Bufs calls its Index method, which returns a copy of the element.
    Buf is an owner type, so the copy would be a second owner of what the
    element owns.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    To look at the element, use a method that returns a pointer to it:
        p := v.Ptr(0)
    To move it out, use a method that removes or replaces it, or take it through
    such a pointer:
        elem := take(v.Ptr(0))
    (see "Indexing library containers" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.11)
    (run "pluto explain E3309" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:43:6: cannot pass one to all as a variadic argument: Buf is an owner type
    Variadic arguments are passed in an array that all sees as a slice. A slice
    is a view and never owns its elements, so one would be released by nobody.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Pass the owners one at a time to a parameter that takes them by value, or
    collect them in an owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Passing arguments to ... parameters" in the Plutonium spec, section
    11.15)
    (run "pluto explain E3310" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:46:10: a slice literal cannot hold owners: Buf is an owner type, and nothing would drop the elements
    A slice literal keeps its elements in an array that the slice only looks at.
    Owners stored there would be released by nobody.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Use an array literal, which owns its elements:
        [...]Buf{...}
    (see "Composite literals" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3312" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:51:3: the deferred call uses f, which is dropped at the end of its block (line 52)
    Deferred calls run when the function returns. f is declared in the block
    that ends at line 52 and is dropped there, before this call would run, so
    the call would use what f had released.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Declare f at the top level of the function, or do the work at the end of the
    block instead of deferring it.
    (see "Defer statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.19)
    (run "pluto explain E3311" for a longer explanation)
